x86_64-linux  clang emits only the C2 variant of a constructor it writes for a class, where cxx1 emits C1 and C2 as it does for all of them - so cxx1's set has _ZN5PlainC1Ev, _ZN5InnerC1Ev and _ZN5OuterC1Ev in it and clang's does not. Nothing here is about member initialisers: those three classes declare no constructor, so the one that applies their initialisers is written by the compiler and is inline by nature with no out-of-line form to switch to. It is the same difference array-of-class.nonames, implicit-move.nonames and move-constructor.nonames already record, and this is the fourth case to meet it for the same reason - a class whose constructor the compiler wrote. C1 and C2 are identical for a class with no virtual bases and both are mergeable definitions, so the extra C1 costs object size and nothing else. arm64-darwin emits both and agrees on all 12 names, and x86_64-windows agrees on all 7.
